														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2446770">
									

<p>Better article overall, so I no longer want to downvote, though I'm not fully convinced of it. I see what you're going for and think the idea is sound, but some parts feel weird and awkward:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This fact remains unchanged even if an individual comes from a region where the term &quot;12th grade&quot; is not applicable.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This feels like an attempt to shoehorn American school systems in the anomalous effects it has on people, regardless of where they come from. Can't they just assume that Mr. ████ is their physics teacher for whatever their equivalent of 12th Grade is?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This consequently leads to a personality change within the host.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I find this whole bit weird, and it's unclear if its due to the memories being altered or Mr. ████ trying to have some effect on the individual to not be tormented by this. If it's the former; do personality changes really need to be the end result? If the latter, why would he do something to that extent?</p>
<p>And my issue with skips that talk about an individual's trauma by focusing on the byproduct of that (like <a href="/scp-1467">SCP-1467</a>) is that we don't know much about the individual, even though the article is really about them. However, unlike that one (which I really like), this doesn't explore his character much, and highlights the effects it has on those infected. It leaves me very wanting.</p>

							</div>

								
					
